@startuml review_11_param_semantics
' Review unit 11 of 15 -- see docs/review_diagrams.md for the series.
' State AFTER commits d2c79af, 870b9de, 7fa9e8e (src +292/-110, tests +383/-113).
' Every number below is from a run of the two cases at commit 7fa9e8e.
' Render: plantuml docs/review_11_param_semantics.puml

title Unit 11 -- var_dims indices and a deterministic density range (7fa9e8e)

skinparam shadowing false
skinparam defaultFontName Monospaced
skinparam defaultFontSize 12
skinparam ArrowColor #555555
skinparam activity {
  BackgroundColor #FCFCFC
  BorderColor #777777
  DiamondBackgroundColor #F2F2F2
  DiamondBorderColor #777777
}
skinparam partition {
  BorderColor #8899AA
  FontColor #33495E
}
skinparam note {
  BackgroundColor #FFFDF0
  BorderColor #C8C08A
}

start

partition "the call -- the same parameters, now read differently" {
  :GenerateData(
     num_obs=100, num_dims=3, ratio_dims=[1, 2, 2],
     <b>density=[0.4, 0.1]</b>, seed=77,
     num_vars=2, <b>var_dims=[3, 2]</b>,
     overlap=0.5, fixed_overlap=False,
     <b>case A</b>: max_obs=None      <b>case B</b>: max_obs=40)
  .generate(netcdf_filepath, parquet_filepath, parquet_tmp, merge_nc);
  note right #C8E6C9
    var_dims may now also be given as explicit index
    lists, e.g. var_dims=[ [0,1,2],[1,2] ], which is the
    form the README's examples use and the form
    MultiVarDimensionsConfig already accepted. Before
    this unit the reference-variable check compared that
    list to num_dims as a number and raised TypeError
    before the code that understood it ever ran.
  end note
}

partition "validators/ -- the two-element density form" {
  #FFCDD2:from_two_element_list: min and max of the pair, and for
  num_vars == 2 a COIN FLIP (rng.random() < 0.5) decided which
  variable got which. Half of all seeds gave var0 the minimum,
  which validate_reference_is_largest then rejected, so the run
  either worked or raised depending on the seed;
  note right #FFCDD2
    Measured at unit 10: seed 77 passes,
    seed 12345 raises
    "ValueError: var0 must be the largest variable
     (or tied for largest) because it is the fixed
     overlap reference".
    Same arguments, different outcome per seed.
  end note
  #C8E6C9:a two-element density is a <b>range written [max, min]</b>
  density[0] is var0's density and must be the largest, else a
  ValueError says so explicitly.
  var0 takes the maximum, one other variable takes the minimum, and
  any further variables are drawn uniformly from the range with
  the DENSITY stream, then the non-reference values are shuffled
  among themselves;
  #C8E6C9:validate_density_refvar no longer overwrites density[0] with
  the maximum, which for a two-element list collapsed the range to
  a single value and gave every variable the same density.
  It also no longer mutates the caller's list;
  #C8E6C9:validate_and_setup_var_dims copies the caller's sequence
  before writing element 0, accepts an int or a list of indices per
  variable, rejects anything else with TypeError, and rejects an
  empty sequence;
  #FFF9C4:for THIS example nothing changes: seed 77 already gave
  var_densities 0.4 and 0.1. The fix is visible as
  <b>seed 12345 going from ValueError to a normal run</b>;
}

partition "configure and place -- unchanged" {
  :var_num_obs 102 and 26, var1 constant on x1 at index 2,
  dim_split 2, per-stratum placement, F1 0.8125, F2 1.0;
}

if (NTASKS) then (== 1  -- CASE A, serial)
  #FFF9C4:no change: ds.nc 15729 B, parquet_0.parquet 6263 B, 118 rows;
else (== 3  -- CASE B, parallel)
  #FFF9C4:no change: three chunk files of 13704 B,
  parquet_0.parquet 5899 B, merged ds.nc 15488 B;
endif

partition "the single-variable flow (num_vars=1, density=0.4)" {
  :untouched: a scalar density never reaches from_two_element_list,
  and var_dims is unused. Files byte-identical;
}

stop

legend right
  |<#C8E6C9> green |= added or changed by THIS unit |
  |<#FFCDD2> red |= removed by THIS unit |
  |<#FFE0B2> orange |= single-variable flow touched by THIS unit |
  |<#FFF9C4> yellow |= generated data or file that changed as a result |
  | plain | unchanged since unit 10 |

  <b>What to review in unit 11</b>
  * that [max, min] is the intended reading of a two-element
    density, and that the README and docstrings now say so
  * the new ValueError when density[0] is not the largest -- it
    turns a previously accepted call into an error
  * the var_dims copy: the old code mutated the caller's list
  * the int-versus-index-list branch, and the TypeError for
    anything else
  <b>Verified</b>: this example is unchanged; a seed that used to
  raise (12345) now runs, with var_densities 0.4 and 0.1.
end legend

@enduml
